WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.640
It used to be that when you were an author,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.618
you hoped your book would hit the&nbsp;
New York Times bestseller list.

00:00:05.618 --> 00:00:08.807
If it did, your book sales would skyrocket.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:14.050
Traditional outlets like the New York&nbsp;
Times once wielded power and prestige.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:19.650
They could influence everything from the books&nbsp;
you buy to the people you vote for, some say.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.010
But the power of mainstream media is waning.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:27.280
Although CNN saw a considerable spike&nbsp;
in viewership during the Capitol riots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.520
as it tends to do when there’s breaking news,&nbsp;
their audience numbers on most days are abysmal.

00:00:34.403 --> 00:00:40.723
When CNN touted in this press release how&nbsp;
it beat out MSNBC in views in April 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.723 --> 00:00:45.000
it ironically revealed that its&nbsp;
own numbers weren’t that great.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.375
CNN’s flagship shows often bring in less than&nbsp;
200,000 daily views in the key 25-54 demographic.

00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:57.055
Yet even their views online are often&nbsp;
less than those of independent YouTubers.

00:00:57.055 --> 00:01:01.295
Why are powerful networks with millions&nbsp;
of dollars of backing behind them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.295 --> 00:01:05.215
getting fewer views than some&nbsp;
individual content creators?

00:01:05.215 --> 00:01:10.255
I thought about my time working as a&nbsp;
television reporter, most recently in London.

00:01:10.255 --> 00:01:13.345
A new day and a new prime&nbsp;
minister, good morning Anne-Marie.

00:01:13.345 --> 00:01:14.945
I’ve compared that with my work now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.960
as an online content creator.
There’s a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:22.200
difference between videos made by TV&nbsp;
networks and videos that are made by YouTubers

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.496
even though both are video-oriented.

00:01:24.496 --> 00:01:27.936
Online content creators have&nbsp;
a lot more creative freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.936 --> 00:01:30.576
which often makes for more compelling content.

00:01:30.576 --> 00:01:34.576
I can make a six-minute video about&nbsp;
the difficulty of landing Starship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.576 --> 00:01:39.136
or a 23-minute video about the dark&nbsp;
web crime kingpin of Silk Road.

00:01:39.136 --> 00:01:42.816
You don’t get the luxury of&nbsp;
that much time with television.

00:01:42.816 --> 00:01:47.357
Take the example of interviews. You might&nbsp;
notice that the format of web-only interviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:51.477
is different from interviews made&nbsp;
for TV that are then released online.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:54.881
When Joe Rogan speaks to guests for&nbsp;
his podcast, the conversation seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.881 --> 00:02:00.000
natural and unscripted - and often&nbsp;
lasts between two to four hours. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.520
Whereas TV interviews are time-sensitive&nbsp;
because they have to move on to another segment.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.668
There has to be solidarity.

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:09.548
I have to end it there because&nbsp;
we're out of time. We'll be back.

00:02:10.048 --> 00:02:15.518
As a result, interviews often sound like&nbsp;
soundbites or may appear inauthentic.

00:02:15.518 --> 00:02:19.753
Even when TV feels authentic&nbsp;
you’re still restricted by time.

00:02:20.963 --> 00:02:25.553
In this hilarious segment from Global News,&nbsp;
the Canadian network where I used to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:30.353
one of the hosts made an artichoke&nbsp;
dip and  it went horribly wrong.

00:02:30.353 --> 00:02:34.889
But there's no vinegar in there.
So here's what happened. What?!

00:02:48.618 --> 00:02:51.208
This was fantastically entertaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.208 --> 00:02:56.038
and could have gone on for longer but they had&nbsp;
to cut to a commercial break or another segment.

00:02:56.038 --> 00:03:02.038
I’ve felt these time constraints myself. My&nbsp;
stories were only 1 minute and 30 seconds long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.038 --> 00:03:07.475
because so many stories have to be squeezed&nbsp;
into the nightly newscast, usually an hour long.

00:03:07.475 --> 00:03:12.275
Time constraints hurt storytelling
because&nbsp;you can’t delve deeply into a topic.

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:15.198
Shows such as 60 Minutes&nbsp;
offer longer-form reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.198 --> 00:03:19.358
but they don’t air daily because they&nbsp;
take considerable resources to produce.

00:03:19.774 --> 00:03:23.534
When daily TV reports are later uploaded online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:27.694
their short, rigid format fails&nbsp;
to compel people to watch.

00:03:27.694 --> 00:03:32.574
For example, my report about a zero-waste&nbsp;
grocery store in the UK may have gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.574 --> 00:03:37.374
hundreds of thousands of views when it aired&nbsp;
on television stations in North America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.374 --> 00:03:43.134
yet it only received 600 views on YouTube&nbsp;
when uploaded by a local Chicago station.

00:03:43.134 --> 00:03:46.988
Or take Bloomberg Technology’s YouTube&nbsp;
channel. They simply reupload segments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.988 --> 00:03:52.914
from their TV program online. The result&nbsp;
is just a few thousand views per video.

00:03:52.914 --> 00:03:57.954
It’s hard for networks to produce content that&nbsp;
works well for online because their storytelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.954 --> 00:04:02.994
is beholden to the format for television&nbsp;
and the advertising dollars it brings.

00:04:02.994 --> 00:04:07.614
You’d be hardpressed to find a network willing&nbsp;
to do an in-depth review of the iPhone SE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:10.828
like on MKBHD’s tech channel, or a 12-minute video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.828 --> 00:04:14.632
about why black balls are floating on a&nbsp;
reservoir like on Veritasium’s science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.632 --> 00:04:20.842
channel because TV audiences don’t have long&nbsp;
attention spans for that kind of storytelling.

00:04:20.842 --> 00:04:27.402
So, why is it that when CNN focused on online&nbsp;
video by launching a streaming service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.402 --> 00:04:29.139
it flopped?

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:32.174
CNN+ was touted as the Netflix of news.

00:04:32.174 --> 00:04:36.894
CNN’s hired hundreds of people and created&nbsp;
dozens of news shows for this service.

00:04:36.894 --> 00:04:43.214
It featured original series, documentaries,&nbsp;
news shows, and live daily programming.

00:04:43.214 --> 00:04:47.057
Instead of the 2 million subscribers&nbsp;
that the head honchos at CNN anticipated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.057 --> 00:04:53.137
after a year, CNN+ reportedly drew&nbsp;
fewer than 10,000 views a day.

00:04:53.137 --> 00:04:57.703
The service shut down in&nbsp;
April after just one month.

00:04:57.703 --> 00:05:00.903
Pretty much no one wanted to pay $5.99&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.903 --> 00:05:05.943
a month to watch news shows when they&nbsp;
can watch videos on YouTube for free.

00:05:05.943 --> 00:05:09.826
Even Netflix is struggling to retain&nbsp;
subscribers partly because of price hikes.

00:05:09.826 --> 00:05:14.786
And again, CNN+’s shows still&nbsp;
maintained the familiar format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.786 --> 00:05:18.325
of a TV broadcast which&nbsp;
doesn’t work well for the web.

00:05:18.325 --> 00:05:23.235
Although the brains behind CNN+ felt it&nbsp;
stood out because of its interactive feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.235 --> 00:05:26.286
where viewers could ask questions,&nbsp;
it really wasn’t anything unique.

00:05:26.286 --> 00:05:30.615
Reddit has been doing that for nearly two decades.

00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:34.215
CNN banked on its brand name to carry the day.

00:05:34.215 --> 00:05:37.517
But it was a humiliating failure.

00:05:37.517 --> 00:05:41.517
Traditional outlets used to be&nbsp;
the gatekeepers of information.

00:05:41.517 --> 00:05:45.437
If you wanted to know what was going&nbsp;
on in the world, you got your news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.437 --> 00:05:48.797
from the radio, your local newspaper, or on TV.

00:05:48.797 --> 00:05:50.957
Now - anybody can be a reporter.

00:05:50.957 --> 00:05:54.877
Some argue this opens the door to disinformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.877 --> 00:05:57.837
as professional journalists are&nbsp;
the ones trained to fact-check.

00:05:57.837 --> 00:06:02.877
However, there are also accusations that&nbsp;
media outlets can’t be trusted, either.

00:06:02.877 --> 00:06:05.997
This media bias chart created by the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.997 --> 00:06:11.037
Ad Fontes Media shows the perceived&nbsp;
political leaning of various news outlets.

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:15.649
Instead of despairing about their demise, media&nbsp;
organizations have to reinvent their format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.649 --> 00:06:20.893
or face certain death when EVERYONE&nbsp;
cuts the cord which is inevitable.

00:06:20.893 --> 00:06:25.533
Social media platforms have made it&nbsp;
possible for individuals to accumulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.533 --> 00:06:31.133
millions of views - even without the backing&nbsp;
of a multi-million-dollar organization.

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:33.773
If you want to learn how to make videos like mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.773 --> 00:06:39.613
you don’t have to go to a fancy school or take&nbsp;
an expensive course. You can learn on Skillshare.

00:06:39.613 --> 00:06:44.093
Skillshare is an online learning community&nbsp;
with thousands of inspiring classes.

00:06:44.093 --> 00:06:47.933
There’s a great class with Ali&nbsp;
Abdaal on how to edit videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.933 --> 00:06:50.849
It’s great for both beginners and those who 
want to brush up on what they already know. 

00:06:50.849 --> 00:06:55.851
Polymatter has amazing classes on how to make 
great thumbnails which are super important.

00:06:55.851 --> 00:06:58.807
You gotta get them to click before they watch. 

00:06:58.807 --> 00:07:05.133
And MKBHD walks you through the whole process from 
start to finish of scripting, shooting, and editing.

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:08.679
There’s something for everyone, 
and you can try Skillshare out for FREE

00:07:08.679 --> 00:07:12.908
by going to my custom link in the
description and pinned comment.

00:07:12.908 --> 00:07:16.856
Thanks for watching. 
For Newsthink, I’m Cindy Pom

